@@ -0,0 +1,1007 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: MPL-2.0
+
+//! USB audio devices as ALSA cards, on the controller's task: each audio
+//! function (`crate::audio`) is a card with a playback and a capture stream
+//! and its feature units' volumes and mutes as mixer controls.
+//!
+//! A stream runs on isochronous transfers: one TD per packet, each in a
+//! bounce buffer of its own. Playback copies a packet's frames out of the
+//! PCM buffer as it queues it, and the stream's position is what it has
+//! queued, with the packets in flight as its delay (Linux's
+//! `snd-usb-audio` does the same); capture copies each packet in as it
+//! arrives. Packets carry the rate's frames per packet, the fraction
+//! carried over (44.1 kHz: 44 and 45 frames); an asynchronous device's
+//! feedback endpoint says its own rate, which playback follows. Every TD
+//! interrupts on completion, most with their interrupt blocked: the event
+//! is written, the task wakes about once a millisecond.
+//!
+//! The PCM layer's calls reach the task through each stream's requests
+//! (prepare waits for its answer; start and stop only say so), and mixer
+//! changes through the mixer's queue.
+//!
+//! Reference: xHCI 1.2, 4.11.2.5 (isochronous TRBs), 4.14.2; Linux's
+//! `sound/usb/{endpoint,pcm,mixer,clock}.c`, `drivers/usb/host/xhci-ring.c`
+//! (`xhci_queue_isoc_tx`).
+
+use alloc::{
+    boxed::Box,
+    collections::VecDeque,
+    format,
+    string::{String, ToString},
+    sync::Arc,
+    vec,
+    vec::Vec,
+};
+use core::sync::atomic::{AtomicBool, AtomicU64, AtomicUsize, Ordering};
+
+use aster_sound::{
